			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">Re: web-design from home, real quick formula for ya, bro (but you have to stick to the plan)
<br />

<br />
- Start with a local service that you like to do.
<br />

<br />
- Link calendars.net, ezboard.com and an index.html to a free site.
<br />

<br />
- Fill that with content of this local service
<br />

<br />
- Gain some hits by telling all of your friends about it
<br />

<br />
- Write e-mails to all of the businesses selling this local service:
<br />
o Inside said e-mail explain that this is a "specialized site which is a service for locals".
<br />
o Include a pricing list (lowball @ first) for linking those companies' sites ads to your site or for creating your own ad for that company
<br />

<br />
- Maintain, maintain, maintain.
<br />

<br />
- When the hits build high enough (and revenue...don't keep it all <img src="images/smiles/icon_razz.gif" alt="icon_razz.gif" border="0" /> ), A.K.A. when the business will pay for it: move it to a local ISP for money.
<br />

<br />
- Rinse, repeat.
<br />

<br />
Here's an example that my wife did for a while:
<br />

<br />
- She likes reading about local department store sales and sharing them with her friends.
<br />
- She set up a calendar with all sales for her friends to plan their shopping weekends and a message board to discuss it all.
<br />
- index.html explained that, had a few neat pics and linked to the above calander and board.
<br />
- All of her friends and friends' friends got on board.  Plus I told all of the ladies @ work.
<br />
- Send e-mails to all of those stores that she is advertising on her site.  (There is a world of possibilities of how to sell ad space to a company when you are bringing them tons of business).
<br />
...
<br />

<br />
You can do it but you really have to love doing that "local service".  You also must keep the maintenance religiously.
<br />

<br />
/salute
